CERBERUS_SMTP_SENDER=your_actual_email@gmail.com
CERBERUS_SMTP_PASSWORD=your_16_character_app_password
CERBERUS_SMTP_RECIPIENTS=your_actual_email@gmail.com
# Q} How to enable email alerts?
# Yaha niche true karna hai original .env file me jab aap email alerts chalu karna chahte ho. Jab aapko alerts nahi chahiye to false kar do.
CERBERUS_EMAIL_ALERTS=false
# for the below if you are in the venv you can use this code in the curly brackets(exclude the brackets) ahead, in the terminal
# use this { python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_hex(32))" } to generate a random 64-character string for the API secret. Copy that string
# and paste it here. 
CERBERUS_API_SECRET=generate_a_random_64_char_string_here

# --- Email Trust/Block links (added this revision) ---
# Q} What is CERBERUS_LINK_SECRET for?
# Signs the "Trust this device" links embedded in alert emails, so they can't be
# forged or tampered with. Generate it the SAME way as CERBERUS_API_SECRET above:
# { python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_hex(32))" }
# If you leave this blank, Cerberus still runs — it just generates a random one
# every time it starts, which means any Trust link from a PREVIOUS run stops
# working after a restart. Set it here once so links stay valid across restarts.
CERBERUS_LINK_SECRET=generate_a_random_64_char_string_here

# Q} What is CERBERUS_PUBLIC_URL for?
# The address YOUR OWN devices can actually reach this machine at — used to build
# the Trust link in alert emails. api_host in config.json is usually "0.0.0.0",
# which is a bind address, not something you can open in a browser. If you leave
# this blank, Trust links default to http://localhost:5000, which only opens
# correctly if you click the link on the SAME computer Cerberus is running on.
# Set this to your machine's LAN IP (find it with `ipconfig` on Windows or
# `ip addr` on Linux) if you want to open Trust links from your phone instead,
# e.g.:
CERBERUS_PUBLIC_URL=http://localhost:5000
CERBERUS_FRONTEND_PORT=8080

# Q} What are CERBERUS_ROUTER_USER / CERBERUS_ROUTER_PASSWORD for?
# Your router's own admin panel login — shown as plain text next to the "Block"
# link in alert emails, so you can copy-paste them in after Cerberus takes you
# to your router's login page. Cerberus does NOT log in for you or block anything
# automatically — it only navigates you to the page and hands you the credentials
# so you don't have to go dig them up yourself.
# Heads up: this means your router password will sit in your email inbox on every
# alert. If that's not okay for your setup, just leave these blank — the email
# will still show the Block link, just without the credentials filled in below it.
CERBERUS_ROUTER_USER=your_router_admin_username
CERBERUS_ROUTER_PASSWORD=your_router_admin_password


# Frontend API Key (must match CERBERUS_API_SECRET above)
VITE_API_KEY=generate_a_random_64_char_string_here

# Q} Why .env.example if we already have .env?
# 1} They serve opposite purposes:

# .env — has your real email, real password, real secrets. Never committed to Git. Never shared. Only exists on your machine.
# .env.example — has the same variable names but empty/fake values. This one is committed to Git.

# 2} Why bother with the second one at all? Because when you eventually push this to GitHub (you've already published CERBERUS v1 on GitHub/Dev.to/LinkedIn)
#  — anyone cloning your repo, including future-you on a new laptop, needs to know which environment variables the project expects, without you having
# to remember and explain it from scratch. .env.example is documentation that says "here are the variables you need to set, copy this file to .env and
# fill them in" — without ever leaking your actual credentials. It's the standard pattern in basically every real-world project for exactly this reason.

# So: .env = your secrets, gitignored. .env.example = the template, safe to publish.

# Things need explanation:

# 1} CERBERUS_SMTP_PASSWORD — this is not your normal Gmail password. Gmail blocks regular passwords for app login. You need a Google "App Password": go
#  to your Google Account → Security → 2-Step Verification (must be ON) → App Passwords → generate one for "Mail". It gives you a 16-character code — that's 
#  what goes here.
# 2}CERBERUS_API_SECRET — leave blank for now. This isn't needed until Module 15 (api/server.py, Phase 3 later). I'll tell you exactly when to generate it.
# 3}CERBERUS_SMTP_RECIPIENTS — can be the same email as sender (you emailing yourself alerts) or a different one. Comma-separate for multiple.
# 4} CERBERUS_LINK_SECRET / CERBERUS_ROUTER_USER / CERBERUS_ROUTER_PASSWORD / CERBERUS_PUBLIC_URL — all new, all optional. Cerberus runs fine with these
#    blank; you only need them if you want the email "Trust"/"Block" buttons to work fully.